(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-sleep-apnea-ohs-vignettes"]={"title":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea and Obesity Hypoventilation Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 54-year-old woman who has taken opioid medications for chronic back pain for years has snoring, frequent awakenings and daytime sleepiness. Which sleep study fits her?","choices":["Home sleep apnea kit","Questionnaire score before any study","Positive airway pressure titration","Polysomnogram in a sleep laboratory"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Long-term opioid use is one of the settings where a polysomnogram, not an at-home kit, is used. Opioid medications are also a risk factor for central sleep apnea, which home kits cannot assess, since they assess obstructive sleep apnea and not other sleep disorders. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 16, 24, 26"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman with obstructive sleep apnea has residual daytime sleepiness despite continuous positive airway pressure, and armodafinil is being considered. She has no heart, liver or psychiatric disease. Which approach to its dose fits her?","choices":["Avoiding the drug because of age","A lower dose than in younger adults","A higher dose than in younger adults","The same dose as in younger adults"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stimulants such as modafinil and armodafinil are given at lower doses in the elderly population. At 78, with no cardiovascular, hepatic or psychiatric condition to add caution, she can be considered for the drug at a reduced dose. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 43-year-old woman with habitual snoring and daytime sleepiness has a home sleep apnea test that does not show obstructive sleep apnea. Her symptoms persist. What is the best next step?","choices":["Modafinil","Polysomnogram","Reassurance","Repeat home test"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. At-home kits will not always diagnose mild obstructive sleep apnea; they are most helpful in moderate to severe disease. A negative home study in a woman with ongoing snoring and sleepiness therefore needs a polysomnogram, which can also find other sleep disorders a home kit cannot assess. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 11"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with newly diagnosed obstructive sleep apnea drinks several glasses of wine each night and takes an over-the-counter sedative to fall asleep. Which advice addresses these modifiable risk factors?","choices":["Keep the wine, stop the sedative","Take both earlier in the evening","Avoid alcohol and other sedatives","Keep the sedative, stop the wine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Use of alcohol or sedatives is a risk factor for obstructive sleep apnea, and the risk modifications are to avoid alcohol and other sedatives, along with diet and exercise for weight loss. Both his nightly wine and his sleep aid fall under that advice. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 15"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman is diagnosed with obesity hypoventilation syndrome. She has coexisting obstructive sleep apnea, and her awake room-air oxygen saturation is 85% (normal 95-100%). Which feature of her presentation points to a poor prognosis?","choices":["Her age in the 60s","Coexisting obstructive apnea","Significant awake hypoxemia","Her female sex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prognosis is poor when there is significant awake hypoxemia at diagnosis and with positive airway pressure therapy. Her awake room-air saturation of 85% is well below normal, and the syndrome already carries high mortality and morbidity, with cardiovascular disease the leading cause of death. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 35, 29, 30"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman developed central sleep apnea after a stroke. She takes no opioids, has normal heart function and lives at sea level. Which therapy is considered first?","choices":["Autotitrating positive airway pressure","Continuous positive airway pressure","Adaptive servo-ventilation","Bilevel positive airway pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of central sleep apnea addresses an underlying cause (heart failure, opioids or altitude), and none applies to her. The next step is to consider continuous positive airway pressure first, with adaptive servo-ventilation held for when it fails. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 24, 12, 33"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman feels tired and sleepy most days, and her husband has seen her stop breathing and gasp during sleep. She does not snore loudly and is not treated for high blood pressure. Her body mass index is 37 kg/m2 and her neck (shirt collar) measures 15 inches. Using the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, which result fits her?","choices":["Score 2, low risk","Score 3, intermediate risk","Score 3, high risk","Score 4, intermediate risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 0 + tired 1 + observed apnea 1 + pressure 0 + body mass index 37 (more than 35) 1 + age 46 0 + neck 15 inches 0 + female 0 = 3. By count that is intermediate, but yes to 2 or more of the first 4 questions plus a body mass index above 35 is a high-risk rule, so she is high risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has loud, habitual snoring, unrefreshing sleep, morning headaches and nocturia. His wife has seen him stop breathing and wake gasping. His blood pressure is poorly controlled on two medicines. His neck circumference is 18 inches, and his posterior oropharynx is crowded. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Narcolepsy","Obstructive sleep apnea","Central sleep apnea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Loud habitual snoring, witnessed apneas, awakening with gasping, unrefreshing sleep, morning headaches, nocturia and poorly controlled hypertension are its history findings, and a large neck and a crowded posterior oropharynx are its examination findings. Narrowing of the upper airway by soft tissue or structure causes it. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 7, 10, 24, 25, 32"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has daytime sleepiness, and her partner reports habitual snoring and pauses in her breathing. Her polysomnogram shows 8 predominantly obstructive respiratory events per hour, and her symptoms are not explained by another condition, a medication or a substance. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Diagnostic of central sleep apnea","Indeterminate until a home kit is done","Diagnostic of obstructive sleep apnea","Not diagnostic until 15 events per hour"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is confirmed with 5 or more predominantly obstructive events per hour plus a symptom such as sleepiness, or habitual snoring and breathing interruptions noted by a bed partner, when no other condition, medication or substance explains them. She has 8 events and those symptoms. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 8, 26"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with obstructive sleep apnea has respiratory events on polysomnogram that occur when he sleeps on his back and not when he lies on his side. He wants a measure to add to his care. Which therapy targets this pattern?","choices":["Nasal steroid spray","Palatal implants","Tracheostomy","Positional therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Positional therapy fits when events occur in the supine position: a sleep belt or sleep shirt keeps him lying on his side. His events are confined to back-sleeping, the exact pattern it addresses. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 13"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a body mass index of 42 kg/m2 has obesity hypoventilation syndrome, confirmed by exclusion. Her husband has witnessed few apneas, and polysomnography with continuous carbon dioxide monitoring shows sleep-related hypoventilation. Which first-line treatment fits her?","choices":["Tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation","Bariatric surgery as first therapy","Weight loss plus bilevel positive airway pressure","Weight loss plus continuous positive airway pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. First-line therapy for obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a weight loss program with positive airway pressure, and the device follows the overlap: with sleep-related hypoventilation it is bilevel positive airway pressure. Her few witnessed apneas, older age and female sex fit that 10% subgroup. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 33, 30, 32"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man started continuous positive airway pressure for obstructive sleep apnea 2 months ago. A repeat polysomnogram on the device shows repetitive apneas in the absence of thoracic-abdominal excursion. Which complication of his therapy has developed?","choices":["Sleep-related hypoventilation","Central sleep apnea","Obstructive apnea despite therapy","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients using continuous positive airway pressure are listed among those at risk for central sleep apnea. Repetitive apneas in the absence of thoracic-abdominal excursion mean airflow stops because effort stops, the polysomnogram finding of central apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 26, 3, 30"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with untreated obstructive sleep apnea has daytime drowsiness and nocturia. His daughter asks whether treatment is worthwhile at his age. What is accurate?","choices":["Nocturia is unrelated to his apnea","Treatment can improve both symptoms","His age makes treatment pointless","His apnea mortality exceeds a younger man's"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mortality from obstructive sleep apnea is lower in the elderly because they compensate, but they still have drowsiness and nocturia, and both improve with continuous positive airway pressure. Treating him targets the symptoms that affect his daily life. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 7"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man with a body mass index of 39 kg/m2 has loud snoring and daytime sleepiness. His awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure is 43 mm Hg (normal 35-45), and no other cause of hypoventilation has been found. How do his results measure against the criteria for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Not met: carbon dioxide not above 45","Met: obesity with snoring is enough","Met: his body mass index exceeds 35","Not met: body mass index is too low"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis needs all three: obesity (body mass index above 30), awake alveolar hypoventilation with an arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mm Hg, and exclusion of other causes. His body mass index of 39 qualifies, but 43 mm Hg is not above 45, so awake hypoventilation is not shown. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 30, 17"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with heart failure reports insomnia, frequent awakenings and nocturnal dyspnea. His wife says he rarely snores, and his breathing at night shows Cheyne-Stokes breathing (hyperventilation alternating with apnea). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Central sleep apnea","Obstructive sleep apnea","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Panic attacks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure is a risk factor for central sleep apnea, especially through Cheyne-Stokes breathing, hyperventilation alternating with apnea from increased sensitivity to carbon dioxide and a prolonged circulation delay to the carotid chemoreceptors. Insomnia, awakenings and nocturnal dyspnea fit, and snoring is less common than in obstructive apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 25, 10, 32"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with untreated obstructive sleep apnea has a fasting glucose of 118 mg/dL (normal 70-99) and triglycerides of 210 mg/dL (normal below 150). Which consequence of her sleep disorder do these results reflect?","choices":["Insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes","Hypothyroidism caused by the apnea","Cortisol deficiency from poor sleep","Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients with obstructive sleep apnea have increased prevalence of insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes and diabetes complications, and the disease is independently associated with increased glucose and triglyceride levels. It may also worsen cardiovascular risk in metabolic syndrome. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 20, 21"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man's polysomnogram shows repeated events in which thermistor airflow falls by 90% or more for 15 seconds while chest and abdominal respiratory effort continue. How is each event classified?","choices":["Hypopnea","Obstructive apnea","Central apnea","Mixed apnea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An apnea is a 90% or greater fall in airflow on the thermistor lasting at least 10 seconds, and when airflow stops but respiratory effort continues, the apnea is obstructive. The airway collapses while the patient still tries to breathe, the event pattern of obstructive sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 24"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has obstructive sleep apnea newly confirmed on polysomnogram, with daytime sleepiness and hypertension. Which treatment is the most effective option for him?","choices":["Nasal expiratory resistance device","Modafinil for daytime sleepiness","Oral appliance worn during sleep","Continuous positive airway pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Continuous positive airway pressure is the most effective non-pharmacologic treatment for obstructive sleep apnea. It also improves consequences of untreated disease, such as reflux, raised proinflammatory cytokines and, in the elderly, drowsiness and nocturia. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 11, 8, 19, 22"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with untreated obstructive sleep apnea has nightly heartburn and symptoms of laryngopharyngeal reflux. Which treatment of her sleep apnea is expected to improve her reflux?","choices":["Positional therapy with a sleep belt","Nasal steroid for congestion","Continuous positive airway pressure","Nasal expiratory resistance device"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. 75 to 100% of patients with untreated obstructive sleep apnea have gastroesophageal reflux disease or laryngopharyngeal reflux, driven by decreased intrathoracic pressure, and the reflux improves with continuous positive airway pressure. Treating her apnea treats a cause of her reflux. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 19, 12, 11"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has a body mass index of 29 kg/m2, daytime sleepiness and an awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure of 50 mm Hg (normal 35-45). How do her results measure against the criteria for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Not met: body mass index not above 30","Met: carbon dioxide is above 45","Met: hypercapnia alone defines it","Not met: carbon dioxide is too low"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome requires obesity (body mass index above 30), awake hypoventilation with an arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mm Hg, and exclusion of alternative causes. Her carbon dioxide of 50 qualifies, but a body mass index of 29 is not above 30, so the diagnosis cannot be made. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 28"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a body mass index of 37 kg/m2 has an awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure of 51 mm Hg (normal 35-45). He has not yet had a chest radiograph, pulmonary function tests, thyroid function tests or a toxicology screen. What must happen before obesity hypoventilation syndrome can be diagnosed?","choices":["Document coexisting obstructive apnea","Confirm a body mass index above 35","Start bilevel pressure therapy first","Exclude other causes of hypercapnia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a diagnosis of exclusion: obesity (body mass index above 30) and awake hypercapnia (arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mm Hg) must be joined by exclusion of alternative causes of hypercapnia and hypoventilation. The workup includes a chest radiograph, pulmonary function tests, thyroid function tests and a toxicology screen. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 30, 31, 33, 17"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with moderate obstructive sleep apnea cannot tolerate continuous positive airway pressure despite several mask changes. Which option is described for his severity and his intolerance of the device?","choices":["Modafinil","Radiofrequency ablation","Palatal implants","Adaptive servo-ventilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Radiofrequency ablation is used in patients with mild to moderate obstructive sleep apnea who cannot tolerate continuous positive airway pressure. His disease is moderate and he cannot tolerate the device, which matches both conditions. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 11, 13, 26"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with loud snoring, witnessed apneas and daytime sleepiness is seen for evaluation before elective surgery. He has never been tested for sleep apnea. Which property makes the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire suited to this visit?","choices":["Highest sensitivity of the questionnaires","Highest specificity of the questionnaires","It confirms the diagnosis before surgery","It grades his disease severity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The questionnaire has been used as a preoperative evaluation tool for people without a known diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea who may be at risk for perioperative complications, and it has the highest sensitivity among the questionnaires. A highly symptomatic patient in a high-risk setting is where it has value. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17, 8, 9"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with a neuromuscular disease causing generalized weakness has snoring and morning headaches. Her awake room-air oxygen saturation is 92% (normal 95-100%). Which study should evaluate her for sleep-disordered breathing?","choices":["Attended polysomnogram","Home sleep apnea kit","Repeat saturation in a month","Pressure titration study"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neuromuscular weakness and awake hypoventilation or hypoxemia are both conditions in which a polysomnogram is used instead of an at-home kit. Her room-air saturation of 92% is below normal, and a polysomnogram can also find sleep disorders other than obstructive sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 31"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man's polysomnogram shows repeated episodes, each 10 seconds or longer, of slowed, shallow, restricted breathing. Airflow is reduced but does not fall by 90%. How are these episodes classified?","choices":["Obstructive apneas","Central apneas","Mixed apneas","Hypopneas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A hypopnea is slowed, shallow, restricted breathing that occurs repeatedly during sleep in episodes of 10 seconds or longer. An apnea needs a fall in airflow of 90% or more for at least 10 seconds, which his episodes do not reach, so these are hypopneas. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman's baseline sleep recording shows mild to moderate sleep-disordered breathing. Her clinician wants to know how severe it is in different stages of sleep and in different sleep positions. Which study fits?","choices":["All-night polysomnogram","Split-night polysomnogram","Home sleep apnea kit","Pressure titration study"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The all-night polysomnogram is performed when sleep-disordered breathing is mild to moderate during the baseline, and it helps determine severity in different stages of sleep and in different sleep positions, the exact question her clinician is asking. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with obstructive sleep apnea has not been helped by continuous positive airway pressure or an oral appliance. He asks which operation would be a cure. Which procedure is described as a cure for obstructive sleep apnea?","choices":["Palatal implants","Radiofrequency ablation","Tracheostomy","Bariatric surgery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tracheostomy is a cure for obstructive sleep apnea because the site of upper airway obstruction is bypassed. Breathing no longer depends on the narrowed upper airway that collapses during sleep. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with loud snoring and fatigue has an 8-hour polysomnogram that records 40 apneas, 32 hypopneas and 40 respiratory effort-related arousals. What is her respiratory disturbance index?","choices":["4 events per hour","5 events per hour","9 events per hour","14 events per hour"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The respiratory disturbance index is (apneas + hypopneas + respiratory effort-related arousals) divided by total sleep time in hours: (40 + 32 + 40) / 8 = 112 / 8 = 14 events per hour. The arousals are obstructive airflow reductions that are neither apneas nor hypopneas. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with a body mass index of 41 kg/m2 and obstructive sleep apnea is being evaluated, and her clinician's suspicion of obesity hypoventilation syndrome is low to moderate. Her serum bicarbonate is 31 mEq/L (normal 22-29). What is the next step?","choices":["Repeat serum bicarbonate","Home sleep apnea test","Diagnose the syndrome now","Arterial blood gas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When suspicion is low to moderate, a serum bicarbonate above 27 means a higher chance of obesity hypoventilation syndrome and the need for an arterial blood gas. Her 31 mEq/L crosses that line, and the gas measures the awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure the diagnosis requires (above 45 mm Hg). — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 31, 8"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man on a mountain trek at high altitude wakes frequently at night. His tent partner notices that his breathing speeds up and then stops, over and over, without snoring. He has no heart failure and takes no medications. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nocturnal asthma","Central sleep apnea","Obstructive sleep apnea","Panic attacks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. High altitude is a risk factor for central sleep apnea, in which increased sensitivity to carbon dioxide produces an unstable pattern of hyperventilation alternating with apnea. Frequent awakenings without snoring fit, since snoring is less common than in obstructive sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 25, 10"}]};
